        <div id="find-the-clog" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Locate the Clog Before You Pick a Tool
          </h2>

          <p>
            Where the water backs up tells you where the blockage sits. If only one fixture drains slowly, the clog is in that fixture's trap or branch line, usually within a few feet of the drain opening. That is the easy case, and a short hand snake or plastic strip will handle it. Bathroom sinks and tubs almost always clog with hair bound together by soap scum, and kitchen sinks with grease and food scraps.
          </p>

          <p>
            If two fixtures on the same branch act up, such as a bathroom sink and tub in the same room, the clog is further down the shared drain line. A 25-foot cable usually reaches it. If the toilet bubbles when the sink drains, or water rises in the tub when you flush, the blockage is in a larger drain or vent that serves several fixtures, and you are moving toward the main line.
          </p>

          <p>
            If every fixture on the lowest floor backs up, or sewage appears in a basement floor drain or shower, the main sewer line is blocked. That is not a job for a hand snake. It needs a drum machine with a three-eighths to half-inch cable, or a sectional machine, and often a plumber with a camera. Stop using water in the house until it is cleared, because every flush adds to the backup.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before reaching for any snake, try the free fixes. Pull the pop-up stopper from a bathroom sink and clean it, because a surprising number of slow sinks are just a hairy stopper. Remove and empty the P-trap under a kitchen sink into a bucket. Use a cup plunger on sinks and a flange plunger on toilets. If those fail, then it is snake time.
          </p>

