        <div id="cable-sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Cable Diameter and Length
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cable diameter is matched to pipe diameter. A <strong>quarter-inch cable</strong> is the standard for sink and tub drains, which are usually one-and-a-quarter to one-and-a-half inches. It bends through traps without binding. A <strong>five-sixteenths</strong> cable is slightly stiffer and suits kitchen lines and two-inch branch drains. A <strong>three-eighths</strong> cable handles two to three-inch lines, and <strong>half-inch</strong> or larger is for three- and four-inch main lines.
          </p>

          <p>
            Using a cable that is too thin in a big pipe is the classic mistake. In a four-inch line a quarter-inch cable just coils up and tangles, or bores a small hole through the clog that refills within days. Going too thick in a small pipe is worse: a half-inch cable will not bend through a sink trap and can crack old fittings. Buy for the pipe you actually have.
          </p>

          <p>
            Length depends on distance to the clog. Fifteen to 25 feet covers nearly every sink, tub and branch drain in a house. Twenty-five feet reaches from most fixtures to the main stack. A main line from a basement cleanout to the street can be 50 to 100 feet, and tree root problems often lie near the property line. Longer cables are heavier and harder to control, so do not buy 50 feet for a bathroom sink.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look at the head too. A simple bulb or spring head grabs hair and pulls it out, which is ideal for bathrooms. Larger machines take interchangeable cutters: spade cutters for grease and soft blockages, C-cutters and saw blades for roots. Hair grabbed and pulled out is a real fix. Boring a small hole through a grease clog is a temporary one, which is why kitchen clogs return.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical pipe and cable pairings</p>
