        <div id="snake-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Types of Drain Snakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Plastic barbed strips</strong> cost a few dollars and solve more bathroom clogs than any other tool. You push the strip down past the stopper, twist and pull, and the barbs bring hair up with them. They reach about 18 to 24 inches, which is enough for most sink and tub clogs that sit in or above the trap. They are not reusable in any pleasant way, so buy a multipack.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hand drum snakes</strong> store 15 to 25 feet of quarter-inch cable in an enclosed drum. You feed cable into the drain, lock it, and turn the drum to spin the head. The drum keeps the dirty cable contained, which is the main improvement over older open coil snakes. Some, such as the RIDGID PowerSpin, accept a drill for powered spinning, which saves effort on long runs.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Cordless drain augers</strong> run on a power tool battery and usually add an autofeed trigger that advances and retracts cable without your hands on it. They are the most comfortable choice for anyone clearing drains often, such as landlords. <strong>Closet augers</strong> are a separate category: a short, stiff cable inside a curved tube with a rubber or vinyl guard, made only for toilets.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Electric drum machines</strong> carry 50 to 100 feet of three-eighths to half-inch cable, a motor, and usually a foot pedal. They are the tool for main lines and roots. They can also injure you if used carelessly, because the cable stores torque and can whip or kink. Renting one costs roughly $50 to $90 a day at the time of writing, which makes sense for a single job. Buying is reasonable only if roots return every year.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-snake" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Snake a Drain Without Making It Worse
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start by opening the right access point. For a bathroom sink, remove the pop-up stopper, or better, take off the P-trap and go into the trap arm in the wall, which avoids fighting the tight bends under the basin. For a tub, remove the overflow plate and feed the cable through the overflow, which gives a straighter path than the drain opening. Put towels and a bucket in place before you start.
          </p>

          <p>
            Feed the cable slowly until you feel resistance. At first, the resistance may be a bend rather than the clog. Lock the cable a few inches from the opening and turn the drum clockwise while pushing gently. A bend gives way and the cable moves on. A clog feels spongy or solid. Keep turning and push into it, then pull back a few inches, and repeat to break it up or hook it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Always keep the cable turning clockwise while you retract it, so it does not unwind. Pull it back slowly and wipe it with a rag as it comes out. If the head brings up a mat of hair, you have cleared the problem. Run hot tap water for a minute or two to confirm the drain is moving freely, and if it slows again, go back in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wear gloves: rubber or nitrile for hand snakes, and the manufacturer's leather mitts for electric machines. Eye protection matters because cables fling dirty water when they spin out of the pipe. With a powered auger, never let the cable spin outside the pipe or without load, because it can loop and whip. Never wear loose cotton gloves with a drum machine; they can catch on the spinning cable.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SAFETY FIRST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If anyone has poured chemical drain cleaner in, do not snake the drain.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Caustic and acid drain openers can splash back off a spinning cable and cause serious burns to skin and eyes. Flush the drain with plenty of water if you can, tell anyone working on it what was used, and let a plumber handle it if the chemicals are still sitting in the pipe.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="toilets" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Toilet Clogs Need Their Own Tool
          </h2>

          <p>
            A standard drum snake in a toilet is a bad idea. The bare metal cable can scratch the glazed porcelain, leaving permanent gray marks, and the cable tends to coil in the bowl instead of working through the trapway. A <strong>closet auger</strong> fixes both problems: the cable runs inside a rigid curved tube with a protective guard, so you can steer it into the trap without metal touching the porcelain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Try a flange plunger first. Toilet plungers with a fold-out rubber flange seal the trap opening, and a dozen firm strokes clears most clogs made of paper and waste. If plunging fails, the clog is usually something solid, such as a toy, a wipe, or a heavy wad of paper caught in the trapway. That is where the closet auger earns its place.
          </p>

          <p>
            Set the auger tube in the bowl with the bend facing into the trap, crank the cable down until it stops, then crank and push gently. When you feel it give, pull back while still cranking to bring the object out. A three- or six-foot closet auger reaches through the toilet's internal trap and into the closet bend below it, which covers nearly every toilet-only clog.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the auger passes through the toilet and the bowl still will not drain, the clog is further down the drain line. At that point, pulling the toilet off the flange and running a drum snake directly into the closet bend gives you access. That means a new wax ring, and if you are uncomfortable doing it, it is a reasonable point to call a plumber.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="main-lines" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Main Lines and When to Call a Pro
          </h2>

          <p>
            Main sewer clogs are usually roots, grease buildup, or collapsed or offset pipe. Roots find their way into clay and older cast iron lines at joints, and they grow back every year. An electric drum machine with a root cutter cuts them back, but it does not fix the joint. Grease buildup from years of kitchen use narrows the line until a small blockage seals it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Always enter a main line through a cleanout, not a toilet or floor drain. A cleanout is a capped fitting, usually in the basement, crawlspace, or outside near the foundation. Open it slowly and stand to the side, because sewage under pressure can rush out. Have buckets and plastic sheeting ready. Feed the machine cable with the foot pedal and keep both hands on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Call a plumber if you hit the same clog more than once or twice a year, if the snake brings back pieces of clay pipe or a lot of roots, or if you suspect a collapsed line. A plumber can run a camera inspection, typically roughly $150 to $500 at the time of writing, to find the exact problem, then quote a lining, spot repair or replacement. Repeated snaking of a broken pipe only delays a larger bill.
          </p>

          <p>
            Call a plumber immediately if sewage is backing into living space, if you have a septic system and suspect a tank or field problem, or if the clog is beyond where you can reach safely. Main line work also comes with sanitation hazards, so wash thoroughly afterward and disinfect any surface sewage touched. If water reached a finished basement, our <a href="/how-to-fix-a-damp-basement/">damp basement guide</a> covers drying out the space.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pipe-safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Protecting Your Pipes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hand snakes are safe in PVC, ABS, copper and cast iron when used with reasonable force. The danger comes from forcing a cable through a joint, spinning a powered auger hard against a bend, or using an oversized cable. PVC fittings can crack if a thick cable is driven through a tight bend, and old cast iron or galvanized lines may be thin from corrosion. If the cable stops dead, back off rather than leaning harder.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chemical drain openers are hard on pipes too, especially older metal ones, and they rarely clear hair clogs completely. They also make any later snaking dangerous. Enzyme or bacterial drain cleaners are gentler and can help with slow buildup over time, but they work over days rather than minutes and do not clear a full blockage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Kitchen sinks with a garbage disposal need care. Snaking through the disposal can damage it. Instead, disconnect the trap or drain line below the disposal and snake from there. Never put your hand in a disposal, and turn off its circuit at the panel before working on it. If you are not sure which breaker feeds it, a <a href="/best-circuit-breaker-finder/">circuit breaker finder</a> identifies it in a minute.
          </p>

          <p>
            Also watch the water you use while testing. Running a lot of water into a still-clogged drain can overflow the fixture, and in older homes it can push water out of hidden joints. Test with a modest flow first. Once the drain clears, run hot water for a couple of minutes to flush away loosened debris.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-vs-plumber" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Snake Cost vs Plumber Cost
          </h2>

          <p>
            A plumber's drain clearing visit typically runs roughly $150 to $350 for a simple fixture clog and $250 to $700 or more for a main line at the time of writing, with after-hours rates higher. Most homeowners hit one or two fixture clogs a year. Against that, a $5 barbed strip and a $25 to $60 hand drum snake pay for themselves the first time they are used.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cordless augers cost roughly $100 to $200 as bare tools and make sense if you already own the battery platform or manage rental units. Electric drum machines for main lines cost roughly $250 to $500 for budget models and well over $1,000 for professional ones. Unless you deal with recurring roots, renting is cheaper and avoids storing a heavy, oily machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also a cost to getting it wrong. A scratched toilet bowl, a cracked PVC fitting, or a cable stuck in a pipe can turn a $200 call into a $1,000 repair. Buy the tool sized for your problem, use the right access point, and stop when you hit something you cannot identify. Knowing when to stop is the cheapest part of DIY plumbing.
          </p>

