        <div id="prevention" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Care and Prevention
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean the cable after every use. Pull it out fully, wipe it with a rag and a mild disinfectant, and let it dry before retracting it into the drum. Leaving wet sludge inside a drum rusts the cable and creates an unpleasant smell. A light coat of oil or the manufacturer's recommended lubricant on a dry cable helps prevent corrosion between uses.
          </p>

          <p>
            Replace a cable that has kinked badly. A kink weakens the coil, and a weakened cable can break inside the pipe, which turns a clog into a plumbing repair. Many drum snakes accept replacement cables, which is cheaper than replacing the whole tool. Store snakes in a dry place, not under a damp sink or in a humid crawlspace.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prevention is cheaper than any snake. Put inexpensive mesh hair catchers over tub and shower drains. Scrape grease into the trash instead of the sink, and do not rely on hot water to carry it away, because it cools and sticks further down the line. Never flush wipes, even ones labeled flushable, as they are a leading cause of toilet and main line clogs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flush slow drains occasionally with a kettle of hot, not boiling, water, especially in metal drains. For PVC, use hot tap water, because boiling water can soften joints. If a drain clogs every few months despite all this, the pipe slope or venting may be wrong, or there may be buildup deeper in the line, and a plumber with a camera can tell you which. Hard water also leaves scale that narrows old metal drains over the years; if faucets and showerheads crust with white deposits, the softening and filtration options in our <a href="/best-water-filtration-system-for-home/">home water filtration system guide</a> deal with the cause.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a sink snake on a main line</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A quarter-inch cable in a four-inch sewer line coils and tangles, or punches a small hole that clogs again within days. Main lines need a three-eighths or half-inch cable on a powered drum machine. If all the lowest drains are backing up, rent the right machine or call a plumber.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a bare cable through a toilet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Standard drum snakes scratch porcelain and leave permanent gray marks, and the cable often coils in the bowl without reaching the trap. A closet auger with a guarded tube costs about the same and is made for the job. Plunge first, then auger. A flange plunger handles most toilet clogs anyway.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Snaking after chemical drain cleaner</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Caustic and acidic drain openers can splash off a spinning cable onto skin and eyes. If chemicals are sitting in the drain, flush with water if possible, warn anyone working on it, and consider leaving it to a plumber. Do not stack chemical and mechanical clearing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forcing the cable through a hard stop</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A cable that stops dead may be at a tee fitting, a tight bend or a broken pipe. Pushing harder can crack PVC fittings or kink the cable. Back off, rotate and try again with gentle pressure. If it still will not pass, try a different access point.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Retracting without spinning</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pulling a cable out without turning it clockwise can let it unwind or drop whatever the head grabbed. Keep turning slowly while retracting, and wipe the cable as it comes out. That brings the clog out with it and keeps the cable from fouling the drum.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which drain snake should a homeowner buy first?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Start with a pack of plastic barbed strips for hair clogs and a 25-foot quarter-inch drum snake such as the RIDGID PowerSpin+ for sinks, tubs and branch lines. Add a closet auger if toilets clog. Those three tools clear most household clogs for well under $100 in total. Rent a machine for main lines.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a regular snake safe in a toilet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not a good idea. A bare metal cable can scratch the porcelain permanently, and it tends to coil in the bowl rather than feed through the trap. A closet auger runs its cable inside a guarded tube made for toilets. Try a flange plunger first, then the auger if plunging fails.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What cable length do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For sinks, tubs and showers, 15 to 25 feet is plenty, since most clogs are within a few feet of the drain. Twenty-five feet reaches most branch lines. Main lines from a cleanout to the street often need 50 to 100 feet and a thicker cable on a powered machine.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does the drain clog again after snaking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually the snake bored a hole through the clog rather than removing it, which happens with grease or with a cable too thin for the pipe. Recurrence can also mean roots, pipe damage, poor slope, or a venting problem. If it returns within weeks, try a cutter head or larger cable, and if it persists, have the line camera inspected.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a snake damage PVC pipes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Used with reasonable force, a correctly sized hand snake is safe in PVC. Damage comes from forcing a cable that is too thick through tight fittings or running a powered auger hard against a bend. If the cable stops dead, back off instead of pushing harder. Old, corroded metal pipes deserve extra care.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is chemical drain cleaner better than a snake?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Chemical openers often fail on hair clogs, can harm older pipes, and make any later mechanical work dangerous because of splash-back. A snake physically removes the clog. Enzyme cleaners are gentler for maintenance but are slow and will not clear a full blockage. A $5 plastic barbed strip often clears a hair clog that chemicals cannot.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I rent or buy an electric drain machine?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rent if the main line clogs once. Rental machines are professional grade and typically cost roughly $50 to $90 a day. Buy a budget machine only if roots or grease clog your main line every year or more. If clogs keep recurring, pay for a camera inspection first. Ask the rental shop for a quick demonstration first.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
